      "text": "To solve the problem of determining the number of ways to arrange 3 math books and 5 English books on a shelf such that all the math books remain together and all the English books remain together, we can follow these steps:\n\n1. **Treat the math books and the English books as single units:**\n   - We have 3 math books and 5 English books.\n   - If we treat the 3 math books as one unit and the 5 English books as another unit, we then have 2 units to arrange on the shelf.\n\n2. **Arrange the units:**\n   - The number of ways to arrange 2 units is given by the factorial of the number of units.\n   - The factorial of 2 is \\(2!\\).\n\n\\[\n2! = 2 \\times 1 = 2\n\\]\n\nSo, there are 2 ways to arrange the 2 units on the shelf.\n\n3. **Arrange the books within each unit:**\n   - The 3 math books can be arranged among themselves in \\(3!\\) ways.\n   - The 5 English books can be arranged among themselves in \\(5!\\) ways.\n\n\\[\n3! = 3 \\times 2 \\times 1 = 6\n\\]\n\n\\[\n5! = 5 \\times 4 \\times 3 \\times 2 \\times 1 = 120\n\\]\n\n4. **Combine the arrangements:**\n   - The total number of arrangements is the product of the number of ways to arrange the units and the number of ways to arrange the books within each unit.\n\n\\[\n\\text{Total number of arrangements} = (2!) \\times (3!) \\times (5!) = 2 \\times 6 \\times 120\n\\]\n\n5. **Calculate the final number of arrangements:**\n\n\\[\n2 \\times 6 \\times 120 = 720\n\\]\n\nTherefore, the final answer is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{720}\n\\]",
